Subject: [PATCH] blink: expose WebBluetooth on Linux so the API surface follows the profile

Upstream gates the Bluetooth interface on the build platform:
runtime_enabled_features.json5 marks WebBluetooth "stable" on Android,
ChromeOS, iOS, macOS and Windows, and leaves "default" — which is what Linux
takes — at "experimental". The interface is therefore absent from the global
scope on a Linux build and present on every platform we ship profiles for.

Conformance found it. The reference M4 Max reports Bluetooth present; our Linux
build reported it absent, while the same capture claimed macOS in the user
agent, navigator.platform, and the full Client Hint set. That combination is
decidable from one page load and needs no reference data: the identity says
macOS, the API surface says Linux. It is a stronger signal than any single
wrong value, because it is an internal contradiction rather than a number a
detector would have to know the right answer for.

Enumerated the rest of the class rather than fixing only what conformance
happened to probe. Of the runtime-enabled features whose status varies by
platform, only two are stable on macOS and not on Linux, and one on Windows:

  WebBluetooth      Android/ChromeOS/iOS/Mac/Win stable, default experimental
  BarcodeDetector   Android/ChromeOS/Mac stable, default test

BarcodeDetector is deliberately left alone. Its own comment points at
//services/shape_detection, which has no Linux implementation, so exposing the
interface would replace a missing API with one that is present and broken —
a worse incoherence than the one it fixes.

WebBluetooth is different: the BlueZ backend exists on Linux, so this exposes a
real implementation. On a host with no adapter getAvailability() resolves
false, which is what the claimed device reports with its radio off. That is
coherent. Driving availability itself from the profile is a separate row and
is not done here.

Ledger: api.bluetooth-presence.
diff --git a/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/runtime_enabled_features.json5 b/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/runtime_enabled_features.json5
index 08261356c8edf..6cd3b54b0cac4 100644
--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/runtime_enabled_features.json5
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/runtime_enabled_features.json5
@@ -7035,6 +7035,20 @@
 
     // WebBluetooth is enabled by default on Android, ChromeOS, iOS, macOS and
     // Windows.
+    //
+    // Apostate also enables it on Linux. Upstream leaves Linux at
+    // "experimental", so the Bluetooth interface is absent from the global
+    // scope there and present everywhere else. That absence is a property of
+    // the host we build on, not of the device a profile claims to be: a
+    // profile describing Windows or macOS hardware served from a Linux host
+    // would be missing an interface the claimed device has, which is decidable
+    // from one page load with no reference data.
+    //
+    // The BlueZ backend exists on Linux, so this exposes a real implementation
+    // rather than a stub. On a host with no adapter getAvailability() resolves
+    // false, which is what the claimed device reports when its radio is off —
+    // coherent, where an absent interface is not. Availability itself is not
+    // yet driven from the profile; see the ledger row.
     {
       name: "WebBluetooth",
       public: true,
@@ -7042,6 +7056,7 @@
         "Android": "stable",
         "ChromeOS": "stable",
         "iOS": "stable",
+        "Linux": "stable",
         "Mac": "stable",
         "Win": "stable",
         "default": "experimental",
